 ttps://luma.com/5ach1gu9\n\nIn the decades following World War II\, Americ
 an social scientists turned to the study of Japanese society as an emergin
 g ally and fellow capitalist democracy. At a time when influential theorie
 s of modernization predicted the decline of kinship\, community\, hierarch
 y\, and collective obligation\, Japan presented a puzzle. It was an advanc
 ed industrial and capitalist society in which village\, family\, corporate
  welfare\, and moral persuasion in public institutions played powerful rol
 es in organizing everyday life.\n\n​This talk examines how postwar schol
 ars treated Japan not simply as an object of study but as a site for think
 ing through a larger problem in modern social thought: the relationship be
 tween individual freedom and social solidarity. Drawing on chapters from a
  larger book project\, I revisit three influential studies of Japan: Ruth 
 Benedict's The Chrysanthemum and the Sword (1946)\, Ezra Vogel's Japan's N
 ew Middle Class (1963)\, and Thomas Rohlen's For Harmony and Strength (197
 4). I argue that these works are best understood not only as interpretatio
 ns of Japan but also as reflections on what I call the "social question" i
 n postwar thought: how modern societies cultivate attachment\, obligation\
 , welfare\, and collective purpose while preserving democratic freedoms an
 d individual autonomy. By placing these texts in conversation\, the talk s
 uggests that postwar Japan functioned as a distinctive archive for thinkin
 g about enduring questions of social reproduction and social solida
